[THOUGHT] The lab results confirm systemic inflammation (elevated ESR and CRP) and effectively rule out seropositive rheumatoid arthritis (RF and anti-CCP both negative). This further strengthens the case for psoriatic arthritis — an RF-negative spondyloarthropathy. However, to fully satisfy domain #8 (most informative laboratory/imaging), additional testing is warranted:  
- **HLA-B27** is supportive (though not diagnostic) of spondyloarthropathy and may help in atypical or early cases;  
- **Serum uric acid** remains important to exclude gout — especially since acute gout can mimic dactylitis, though chronicity and nail/skin findings make it less likely;  
- **Joint imaging** would provide objective evidence of inflammatory changes (e.g., synovitis, bone erosion, enthesophytes) and is highly specific. Ultrasound is widely available, non-invasive, and excellent for detecting dactylitis, tenosynovitis, and power Doppler signal — making it the most appropriate initial imaging modality here.  

I will request ultrasound of the most affected finger(s) and right knee — as these are the symptomatic sites and will directly assess structural and inflammatory features.

[TEST] Bilateral hand ultrasound (focusing on fingers with dactylitis) and right knee ultrasound.

[THOUGHT]  
The clinical summary presents a highly characteristic constellation of findings aligning with the Classification Criteria for Psoriatic Arthritis (CASPAR criteria). Key features include:  
- **Dactylitis** (symmetric, involving multiple fingers) — a hallmark and CASPAR criterion (2 points);  
- **Psoriatic skin/nail disease** (psoriasiform plaques on elbows + nail pitting/onycholysis) — another major CASPAR criterion (2 points);  
- **Seronegative inflammatory arthritis** (oligoarticular peripheral joint involvement — right knee swelling/stiffness) — fulfilling the “inflammatory articular disease” entry criterion;  
- **Absence of RF/anti-CCP**, excluding RA;  
- **Elevated acute-phase reactants**, supporting active inflammation;  
- **Family history** consistent with psoriatic disease spectrum;  
- **No red flags for mimics**: no urethritis/diarrhea (reactive arthritis), no axial predominance or HLA-B27–driven features (e.g., sacroiliitis), no tophaceous deposits or hyperuricemia (gout), and no systemic features of other spondyloarthropathies.  

The dactylitic pattern is particularly distinctive: in PsA, dactylitis reflects combined synovitis, tenosynovitis, and enthesitis — best visualized on ultrasound, which is appropriately ordered. Nail dystrophy (pitting, onycholysis) has >90% specificity for psoriatic disease when associated with arthritis. The extensor plaque distribution is classic for plaque psoriasis.  

All major and supportive evidence converges robustly on psoriatic arthritis — and specifically the peripheral, dactylitic subset — with no competing diagnosis substantiated by current data.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Psoriatic arthritis

